course: dictionary
lesson: 2756
title: Disbelief: when you cannot believe it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Disbelief is the feeling of not believing something, because it is so surprising or shocking. It is a noun, so you can feel disbelief or stare in disbelief. Context often shows it: a character's face, actions and words tell you how they feel.
explain: In a story, «in disbelief» tells you a character cannot believe what they see or hear. Look at what happens around the phrase. Did something amazing or terrible just happen? Then disbelief is the feeling that fits.
explain: The Chinese phrase «bù huì ba» («BOO HWAY ba») means no way, you're kidding. It shows disbelief, not a refusal. The usual reply is «zhēn de», which asks Really? So the words around it tell you the feeling.
fact: Disbelief is a noun: the feeling of not believing that something is true or real.
fact: «In disbelief» is a phrase: a character stares in disbelief when something amazing or terrible happens.
fact: «Shake your head in disbelief» means you shake your head because you cannot believe what you have seen or heard.
fact: «Suspend disbelief» means to accept a story's magic while you read it.
fact: Disbelief can also mean a lack of belief, for example in God.
exercise: «Tom stared at the score in disbelief.» How did Tom feel? | Very hungry | *Shocked, unable to believe it | Proud of a long walk | Bored and sleepy
exercise: What does «suspend disbelief» mean? | To shout at the characters | To stop reading a book for ever | To read the last page first | *To accept a story's magic while you read
exercise: Which action shows disbelief? | Eating a big lunch | *Shaking your head slowly with your mouth open | Running to the door to leave | Yawning in a boring lesson
test: «Bù huì ba!» is what you say when you hear amazing news. What does it show? | Anger at a rude friend | Tiredness after a long day | *Disbelief, you're kidding | Thanks for a gift
test: What does disbelief mean? | *The feeling of not believing something is true | A short rest after work | Being sure that something is true | A feeling of being very happy
exam: She read the letter and shook her head ___. | in a hurry | in silence | *in disbelief | in delight
